<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n38267" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pressĭo</span></b> , ōnis,
			f.
		premo. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>, <i>a pressing</i>, <i>pressing down</i>, <i>pressure</i>
			(only in Vitruvius): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pressio cacuminis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vitr.
					10, 8</span></i>
			. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, <i>the prop</i> or
				<i>fulcrum</i> under a lever while the burden is
			raised, <i><span class="en">Caes. B. C. 2, 9, 6</span></i>: pressio, quod
			Graeci <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὑπομόχλιον</span> appellant, <i><span class="en">Vitr. 10,
			8</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
